.TH CCOL_CIRCULAR_QUEUE_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_circular_queue_create \- create a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_circular_queue_create(max_size, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a queue with a fixed size. Inside, the queue uses a
circular buffer. A send blocks while the queue is full. It waits for free
space. The macro uses the standard malloc and free for memory management.

This macro is the short form of ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs()
with NULL memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size  Maximum number of messages that the queue can hold. The value
          must be from 1 to SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t). The macro
          refuses a larger value. One array of
          max_size * sizeof(c_message_t) bytes holds the queue, and a
          larger value makes that computation overflow.
err_str   Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the macro gives a pointer to the new circular queue.
On failure, it gives NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe.
You must destroy the queue with ccol_circular_queue_destroy() after you
finish with it.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with this queue. The first
way is to call fork(2) before you create the queue. The second way is to
create the queue, then call fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One
pattern is not supported: a program continues to run with no exec(3) in
between while a queue from before the fork is still live. On top of that
rule, the library adds a defense in depth. The child never inherits a lock
in the locked state from another thread that held it at the instant of the
fork. Also, the queue owns no thread of its own. A queue that a child
inherits in that way therefore stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR ccol_circular_queue_destroy (3)
